Vendored source: Proton's lsteamclient
======================================

Everything under `proton/` is copied verbatim from Valve's Proton, except for
three edits described below, each marked in place with a comment beginning
"ppc64le port:".

    upstream   https://github.com/ValveSoftware/Proton
    tag        proton-11.0-1b
    commit     0745bfbc4cf4365e8cf048b003990c59def29948
    subtree    lsteamclient/   (vendored here as dlls/steamclient64/proton)
    copied     2026-08-17

Proton's own licensing applies to those files and their headers are unchanged:
`proton/LICENSE` is the Steamworks SDK licence covering `proton/steamworks_sdk_*`,
and the wrapper sources carry Wine's LGPL 2.1 headers.  Nothing here is
redistributed; this is a build tree.

Everything OUTSIDE `proton/` is this port's own, LGPL 2.1 like the rest of the
tree:

    steamrpc_wire.h        the frame format between the guest DLL and the helper
    steamrpc_private.h     the marshal-descriptor shapes
    steamrpc.c             the client: descriptors -> frames -> socket
    steamrpc_generated.c   the descriptors, from tools/steamrpc/gen-steamrpc
    steamrpc_count.h       ditto, the method count both ends C_ASSERT on
    steamrpc_chkstk.c      the stack probe clang emits for a large frame
    steamclient_guest.h    what Proton's PE side needs to compile for x86-64
    steamclient64.def      the export list, ordinals from proton/lsteamclient.spec
    steamclient64.guestpe  the build-lane control file


Why the vendored tree is used THREE TIMES
-----------------------------------------

Proton's lsteamclient has two halves either side of a `WINE_UNIX_CALL`
boundary, and this port compiles them for two different machines:

  * the PE half (`proton/steamclient_main.c`, `proton/winISteam*.c`,
    `proton/steamclient_generated.c`, the manual wrappers) becomes the guest
    x86-64 Windows DLL `steamclient64.dll`, so that a game's Steamworks vtable
    calls are x86-64 calling x86-64 exactly as they are under Proton;

  * the unix half (`proton/unixlib*.cpp`, `proton/cppISteam*.cpp`,
    `proton/unix_steam_*.cpp`, `proton/steamworks_sdk_*`) is compiled by
    `ppc64le/steamapi/helper/build-helper.sh` into an x86-64 **Linux**
    executable, because the real `~/.steam/sdk64/steamclient.so` is an x86-64
    SysV ELF that native ppc64le Wine cannot load.

  * the PE half is compiled a SECOND time, for i386, as the guest
    `steamclient.dll` -- the name a 32-bit game's own `steam_api.dll` asks
    for.  That build lives in `dlls/steamclient`, which is two files
    (a .guestpe and a .def) pointing back here; there is no second copy of
    any source.  Proton does the same thing, shipping i386-windows and
    x86_64-windows builds of one tree, which is why nothing under `proton/`
    needed a 32-bit edit: the width-dependent parts are already behind its
    own `#ifdef __i386__`.

Both halves are otherwise Proton's code doing Proton's work.  Only the
boundary between them moved -- from a call within one process to a socket
between two.  NOTE that the i386 PE half has no unix half to talk to yet: the
helper is an x86-64 program and a frame is the params struct's own bytes, so
serving 32-bit games for real needs an i386 helper against Steam's own 32-bit
steamclient.so, exactly as Proton ships i386-unix/lsteamclient.so.


The three edits to vendored files
---------------------------------

1. `proton/steamclient_main.c`, `get_unix_buffer()`.  Proton short-circuits
   when the returned `u_buffer` pointer fits a `UINT_PTR`, because its unix
   half shares the address space.  Here that pointer is a helper address which
   always "fits" and never points at anything, so the short circuit is removed
   rather than made conditional.

2. `proton/steamclient_main.c`, `DllMain`.  One added call publishing
   `ActiveProcess\pid`; see the comment there and in `steamrpc.c`.

3. `proton/unixlib.h`, the `STEAMCLIENT_CALL` macro.  Proton `assert()`s that
   a unix call never fails.  Across a process, "the helper is not there" is
   the ordinary state of a game launched outside Steam, and the assert would
   abort the game instead of letting it print its own SteamAPI_Init failure.

